  "documentation": {
    "range": "<em>Libellula depressa</em> is a largely western Palaearctic species which is widespread in Europe and ranges east to Central Asia. The species is common and widespread in most European countries but is absent from Ireland and confined to the southern third of Fennoscandia.",
    "population": "It is one of the most common and widespread species of Europe and Southwest Asia. This is probably also true for the other parts of its Asian range.",
    "habitats": "<em>Libellula depressa</em> is found in largely unshaded, standing and slow-flowing waters, often occurs in small, shallow, sunny water bodies and biotopes in early succession stages. The species is often one of the first to colonize new man-made waters.",
    "threats": "<p>No threats of importance to its global range are known. </p>",
    "measures": "<p>&#160;No conservation measures needed.</p>",
    "use_trade": "This species is not utilized.",
    "rationale": "<p>The species is common within its wide range and there are no indications of a decline. </p>",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "In some literature, this species has been assigned to the genera <em>Platetrum</em> or <em>Ladona</em> (Schmidt 1987) but presently most authors use the <em>Libellula</em> as genus name."
  },
